  {
    "episode_id": 89156,
    "turn_file": "/shared/3/projects/podcasts/transcriptionQueue/turns/pol_appearance_episodes_interviews/8/9/89156.jsonl",
    "turn_idx": 16,
    "turn_text": "A lot of my friends asked me that. Well, I was 21, and I decided to run for this state house. And for me, it was growing up in Hawaii. I loved being in the ocean more than I was comfortable being on land. I loved surfing and being outdoors and just appreciating our home. And I understood that My own having fun came with responsibility. And that responsibility is to be a caretaker. And that was really what sparked my interest in getting involved and trying to find ways to be a good caretaker. And there were a number of different things. I co-founded an environmental nonprofit, I don't know, I was 17 or so. called Healthy Hawaii Coalition and created this program that we took out to elementary school students all across the state. And I had so much fun hanging out with these kids and just talking to them about some of the basics of like, hey, when you're driving down the street and you eat in a bag of chips and you throw your bag of chips outside the window of your car, this is where it ends up. You like when playing the water and just the basics. There are consequences to the actions that you take. And seeing that kind of light spark in their eyes was just the coolest thing, seeing that they actually understood what I was talking about. There were other things that we did to organize, get petition signed to stop landfill from being built over one of the biggest water aquifers on our island, the water that serves almost a third of the population. on our island and going through that, standing outside the grocery store as a teenager, you know, getting these signatures and then seeing how politicians were trying to grease the wheels to fast track this landfill project for their buddies even though they recognize man like that landfill starts leaching toxins down into the groundwater. That's a bad deal because we live on an island and that is where our water comes from. We can't truck it in from anywhere else. So we stopped it. We stopped it from happening. And it was an inspiring moment to see how to make that change. And that's why I ran for State House because I saw an opportunity, well, hey, I can stand on the outside and talk about it, or I could go to school. I didn't have a bachelor's degree at the time. I had started going to community college, but I could go and sit in the classroom and listen to a political science professor and have philosophical discussions and debates or I could actually just go and try to actually do something. And so I",
    "speaker_id": "SPEAKER_06",
    "inferred_speaker_name": null,
    "inferred_speaker_role": null,
    "chosen_text_key": "transcript",
    "explicit_propositions": [
