  {
    "episode_id": 89156,
    "turn_file": "/shared/3/projects/podcasts/transcriptionQueue/turns/pol_appearance_episodes_interviews/8/9/89156.jsonl",
    "turn_idx": 18,
    "turn_text": "Yeah, logistics and commuting to work takes on a whole different picture when you're talking about literally traveling, I don't know, 2600 miles one way to get from my home in Hawaii to Washington, D.C. And so it, you know, I did my best to make the most of the time, all of it. Both the time that I had at home, I went back and forth probably at least a couple times a month. I wasn't able to get back every single weekend, like most people. because I'd end up being in the air more than I'd actually be at home, but making the most of the time, it never got easy. So I served in Congress for eight years with you, and it never got easier to leave Hawaii, but I always You know, the reason I did, I understood my purpose. I never forgot, you know, why I ran for Congress in the first place, which really came from the experience that I had as a soldier. I enlisted because of 9-11, because I felt a sense of responsibility and duty and call to action to serve our country and to go after those who attacked us on that day. I also was committed to serving our state so enlisted in the National Guard and then our unit was activated very quickly after I got back from basic training and I was I was not on the mandatory deployment roster because the job I was trained in in a medical unit was already filled by someone else. And so my commander called me and he said, hey, Tulsi, good news. You don't have to go to Iraq with us. This was in kind of the summer of 2004. And I said, well, what do you mean, sir? He's like, yeah, you don't have to go. So-and-so is going to fill with you. She's already filled a position. And so you're set. You can stay home. And I was campaigning for reelection to the State House at the time. I knew there was no way I could stay back. I knew there was no way that I could stand there on the tarmac and wave goodbye to my fellow soldiers and my friends as they shipped off to war on the other side of the world. I told my commander, no, there's no way I'm staying back. And he understood after a few minutes of talking that I meant it. So I withdrew from my reelection campaign and ended up getting retrained in a different job that they needed filling in that medical unit. And we deployed, we were in Iraq for a year in a place in the country at that time. It was taking a lot of casualties. It was about 40 miles north of Baghdad in 2005. And working in a medical unit every day was just as we all were confronted with the high cost of war. It was my daily task every single morning to go through a list of names of servicemen and women who had been injured or harmed in battle the day before and to go through and make sure that any of our soldiers from our brigade combat team basically to make sure that they were getting the care that they needed, either to return to duty or to make sure that they actually got all the way back home to their families. And every day, it was a heavy moment every day, just sometimes seeing names on that list who were people who I knew personally, people who may have been in a different place, seeing many names of people I didn't know personally, but knowing that You know, their loved ones and family members back home as I knew my parents were dreading having a phone call come to let them know that their son or their daughter or their other sister husband or wife had been injured or or worse killed. It was a reminder again about how temporary this life is and to make the most of it, you know, that this is not a real home. We are eternal spirits. We are eternal souls, the children of God, and we have this short window of time here in this life. to make the most of it. And coming back from that deployment, that first deployment, I knew that I wanted to be in a position to do something, to have influence, to be able to help make the decisions about our foreign policy, about our men and women in uniform and our veterans, recognizing that so many of our politicians either don't know, but they're not aware of the consequences and decisions that they're making and the toll that it takes and the costs on human life and on our national security. or worse, they know, but they don't care. And so I didn't, again, know exactly how or where I'd find a way to be able to be impactful in that, but ultimately that was compounded by my experience in my second deployment and ultimately what led me to run for Congress. And it's what I carried and carry with me every day that I get on the plane and leave my home, that I go to Washington or that I go to other places to continue to to try to be of service and to try to bring about this kind of positive",
