    "turn_file": "/shared/3/projects/podcasts/transcriptionQueue/turns/pol_appearance_episodes_interviews/8/9/89156.jsonl",
    "turn_idx": 21,
    "turn_text": "Yeah, I think, uh, I think without question, it would be different. Certainly bring, uh, the perspective that so many people across this country really like, you know, for, for our generation since 9 11 have experienced, uh, but it goes back further than that. Those who served in the Gulf War and Vietnam and Korea and, you know, we just observed the 81st anniversary of the attack on Pearl Harbor. Those who have seen and experienced the ugliness and the tragedy and the harshness of war are often, not always, but often the loudest voices calling for restraint and calling for peace, calling for caution, making sure that if we are going to war, that we are doing so to fulfill a mission that best serves the interests of the American people in our national security. This is not to say that, you know, you mentioned a few of our colleagues in Congress who served and they've deployed and this is not to say that every one of us agrees on every piece of foreign policy. But again, those who have had these experiences are often far more thoughtful",
