    "turn_file": "/shared/3/projects/podcasts/transcriptionQueue/turns/pol_appearance_episodes_interviews/8/9/89156.jsonl",
    "turn_idx": 40,
    "turn_text": "I know exactly the conversation you're talking about. But you weren't afraid. I mean, hey, let's talk about it. I will tell you what that conversation was about because it was a memorable one. It was about an immigration bill. It was not about the presidential race that was happening that year. And I was vice chair of the DNC at the time. And there was a big immigration vote that was coming up. And while I don't remember the exact details of that specific vote, In reading the bill, it was a Republican bill, and the Democrats were trying to get Democrats to vote against it. The Democrat leadership won, Democrats vote against it. And in my reading of the bill, I saw, hey, this bill may actually help secure the border. And that's something that I care about doing. I think it's very important. And so I, you know, as you know, the leadership on both sides, they go around, they ask people, hey, how you plan to vote. And I said, yeah, you know, I'm thinking of voting for it. And as you can imagine, that caused some people's hair to light on fire, just on its face. First of all, let's just talk about that. Just on its face like, oh, oh my gosh. How could she even think about voting for a Republican bill? Which says so much about what's so wrong in Washington. And I'm sure you guys have gotten it on your side as well on different issues. But it was just like, how dare you first of all? So, Steny Hoyer, who we both love, is such a kind person. He came to talk to me about it. And in that conversation, he's like, well, Tulsi, let me know. Like, I'm just curious what's your thought process and why are you thinking of voting for it? And so I told him we were having a good substantive conversation about the content, not partisanship, but on the content of this bill. And then Debbie Wasserman Schultz came up and she was very angry. She was very upset. I had no idea that you were in this space that you saw this go down. She was very, very upset. And she had a lot of words to say to me about why she was upset that I would even consider voting for this Republican bill on immigration. of the things she said had anything to do with the substance of the bill. But the gist of kind of what she was getting across to me was How dare you do this as a vice chair of the DNC? Don't you know this is going to have such a negative impact as vice chair of the DNC, meaning a negative political impact? I'm fairly certain she wasn't concerned about negative political impact on me, but for the DNC. And when she said that, you know, because I stood there and I listened to what she was saying, Oh, as soon as she said that, I don't remember exactly what I said. But what I was trying to get across was you don't know me at all. And I turned back to Steny Hoyer and we continued to have a substantive conversation on the legislation. You know, that's what I was laughing at the beginning of this. There's so many different interactions that I've had with people. And you know, the meaningful ones, the ones that are actually constructive are ones where you can talk. And we may not end up on the same side of an issue, but I will have walked away learning something more about the other person and where they're coming from. And they will have walked away learning more about why I was doing the thing that I was doing. But just, you know, the blind partisan strife and divisiveness and how dare you, you know, there was another day, something was going on, another big, another big vote had happened. And right after the vote happened, this is when John Boehner was speaker. As he left, he was about to leave the house floor, walking down the middle of the aisle by the big doors in the middle. I had seen him doing yoga that morning in the gym. As he was walking down, I said, speaker, good for you for doing yoga this morning. That was great. Lifted my hand, gave him a high five. He had a big smile on his face and he walked out. I turned around, there was one of our other colleagues, senior Democrat, scowling at me, scowling at me. And as she walked away, I heard her talking to her age, like, how could Tulsi do that on a day like this with a boat like this? How dare she? It's like, come",
