    "turn_file": "/shared/3/projects/podcasts/transcriptionQueue/turns/pol_appearance_episodes_interviews/8/9/89156.jsonl",
    "turn_idx": 50,
    "turn_text": "I think the key difference, Trey, is the legal immunity that they received under Section 230, that they received this protection that the New York Times and Politico do not have. As they are publishers, they're responsible for curating what they quote unquote print, whether it's online or on paper. And they are held legally responsible for the words they put on those pages or on their website. For some obvious reasons, if Twitter, Facebook, or Instagram, or these other social media platforms are the kind of public digital square, digital marketplace of ideas that they claim to be, that was the concept, I think, behind section 230, is that they are a platform for people to go in and say what they want to say, period. I can't do that with the New York Times. I can't do that with any other media platform. And that's the real difference there is that when you look at how Twitter, for example, as we're seeing with the release of Elon Musk's release of these Twitter files, we've obviously seen it from Facebook or meta. We obviously see it a lot with Google. We see how these private corporations who are essentially monopolies, because there's no real competition. how they are using their platforms to boost certain voices or messages in silence and censor others. And even more directly and dangerously, how they are doing this in order to influence our elections. And as we're seeing more and more, there's evidence that they are not only doing this on their own to influence our elections, but they have been proven to have been doing this at the behest of people who work in the White House, people who work in high positions of power in the government. who say, hey, yeah, here's a story we want you to kill or not cover. Here's a voice we want you to silence or here's an account we want you to restore. And so this is where it's incredibly dangerous to see this kind of abuse of power and how, and I personally have been an advocate of breaking up big tech because these monopolies are they are way too powerful and are virtually untouchable. I saw how they tried to impact my own campaign when I ran for president. I was the most searched candidate on the first presidential debate night back in 2019. We had a Google ads account set up hoping that that would be the case because not many people in the country or no one in the country really knew much about who I was if they knew me at all. So we hoped they'd be able to go on and You know, type after the debate. Who's Tulsi Gabbard? Learn more about me. Well, Google suspended our ad account with no explanation, wouldn't answer calls or emails or anything to tell us like, okay, what did we do wrong? Let's get this back up on track as quickly as possible to maximize this opportunity. never got back to us at all. And then at whatever point they decided it made sense to them, our account was magically restored. There are so many different points of evidence of the bias that they have For example, in sending a majority of Republican candidates email blasts to the junk folder while sending a majority of Democrat mass email blasts into people's Gmail inboxes. Big tech, private corporations having this much power to directly influence people and control what information people are getting is an incredibly dangerous thing, and they're doing it all with this legal immunity and protection covered by Section 230.",
    "speaker_id": "SPEAKER_06",
    "inferred_speaker_name": null,
