    "turn_file": "/shared/3/projects/podcasts/transcriptionQueue/turns/pol_appearance_episodes_interviews/8/9/89156.jsonl",
    "turn_idx": 54,
    "turn_text": "actually. It was a very, it was a very stark change in difference going from, you know, when I was first elected to Congress, there was a lot of excitement. And unfortunately, it was I mean it was a lot of excitement essentially around the bullet points of my bio. And I say, unfortunately, because it really kind of focused on a lot of the superficial things, rather than focusing on the substantive things, the superficial things. Hey, look, she's a young woman. She's a woman of color. She's a veteran. She's this and that, just going down the list. And for a time, a short time, there was a lot of excitement there. from the media as well as the Democratic Party, but very quickly once people started to hear and listen to the things that I was saying, the issues that I was raising, I think one of the first major ones happened just barely six or seven months after I was sworn in as a member of Congress when I was the first Democrat to come out in opposition to President Obama's desire to go or his seeking authorization from Congress to go and bomb Syria. And it was exactly that kind of momentum situation. That was why I ran for Congress in the first place. I went in and I did my research. I did my due diligence. I listened to all of the testimony from the administration, all of the clothes and open door briefings. did not go in pre-zooming a decision but made my decision after doing the work and came out in opposition to the president's proposal and saying why I thought it would be bad for our country and it would undermine our own national security and get us into yet another quagmire of a war like the war in Iraq. And it was after making that statement that I heard almost immediately back from the White House, and how dare you do this, not because they had disagreement on the substance of my argument against their position. But how dare you do this as a Democrat coming from the president's home state? How dare you come out in opposition to his plan? And from there, and it went on and on and on, there started to be that palpable shift. really because I think they recognize I was somebody who actually thought for myself and wasn't going to just take their talking points and be a parrot for whatever the narrative of the day was and that I was not afraid to step out and do what I believed was the right thing to do for the American people in the country, even if it meant going against what was popular or going against what. the quote-unquote party",
