  {
    "episode_id": 89156,
    "turn_file": "/shared/3/projects/podcasts/transcriptionQueue/turns/pol_appearance_episodes_interviews/8/9/89156.jsonl",
    "turn_idx": 62,
    "turn_text": "God and God's love, no matter how we choose to worship or what name we choose to call God by, there is one God. He has many names. His love for each and every one of us is unconditional. And I will never forget being there that day because it was a day of such tragedy and sorrow. for, especially for the families who had lost loved ones in that tragic shooting. But we all shared in that sorrow. And I was so moved by the words that I had heard from some of those moms as they spoke of their sorrow, but most powerfully of their forgiveness. really truly their forgiveness in the wake of this incredible and unimaginable tragedy of having their loved one taken from them in church by a murderer. And there's only one place that that kind of forgiveness can come from, and that comes from God. and his love. And so, you know, for me, I grew up, you know, my mom was raised Methodist, and then she found the practice of Sadhana Bhakti through Hinduism and scriptures from India. My dad was raised Catholic and still he's elected at his church in Hawaii, but they growing up was inspired by it and learned from and drew inspiration from really two main scriptures for me and that was the New Testament and the Bhagavad Gita, which is a scripture that means Bhagavad Gita means the song of God and it's an ancient Hindu scripture. But the essence of the New Testament and the Bhagavad Gita is essentially the same, which is encapsulated with Jesus Christ's first commandment. Love the Lord your God with all your heart, all your mind, all your spirit. And this practice of said, Hana Bhakti, it means developing a loving relationship with God. and trying to do your very best to be pleasing to him with your life and being able to in that setting and in praise and worship and singing and dancing and clapping about God's love is this it brought the same peace and comfort and joy to my heart that that I have had and found throughout my life through my own spiritual practice, which is focused on prayer and even singing different names of God and praise and worship in some ways. This is the peace and comfort that I found when I was in Iraq and going to sleep most nights with mortar rockets. It was the comfort and peace that I found when, you know, friends of mine were killed in combat. It is the peace and comfort that came from God and the scriptures and God's names that kind of formed a protective shield around me in the swamp of Washington where you can't really trust anybody. And it's what brings me my deepest happiness now.",
    "speaker_id": "SPEAKER_06",
    "inferred_speaker_name": null,
    "inferred_speaker_role": null,
